<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Rustamid imamate</h1><p class="st-meta">777 CE – 909 CE · capital Tahert</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ibadi imamate ruled from Tahert in Algeria, known for learning, trade and religious tolerance in the early Islamic Maghreb.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ibn-rustam">Abd al-Rahman ibn Rustam</a>, of Persian descent, had governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ibadi-imamate-of-tripolitania">Ibadi Imamate of Tripolitania</a>. After its defeat in 761 he fled west and founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a> (near modern Tiaret), where around 777 the Ibadi community elected him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In theory the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> was elective, but in practice the Rustamids ruled hereditarily, advised by religious scholars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a> attracted merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and its scholars debated theology freely. The state relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes and Ibadi communities as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jebel Nafusa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-ouargla">Ouargla</a>, and profited from caravan trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aflah-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Aflah ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> ruled for about fifty years.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 909 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a>, leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a> armies of the Fatimid movement, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a>. The surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadis</a> fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-ouargla">Ouargla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sedrata">Sedrata</a>, and later to the Mzab, where the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mzab-confederation">Mzab confederation</a> preserved their tradition.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rustamid-imamate.jpg" alt="Rustamid imamate" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: R Prazeres · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rustamid_territory_9th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>777 CE – 909 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tahert</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd al-Rahman ibn Rustam, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Rahman, Aflah ibn Abd al-Wahhab</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rustamids, Rustamid dynasty, Imamate of Tahert</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=843&amp;focus=rustamid-imamate" aria-label="Rustamid imamate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4713 899 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rustamid imamate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5033 1155L5338 1239M5033 1155L5811 1337M5033 1155L5094 1253"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5811 1337h0M5094 1253h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5033 1155h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5033 1155h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=843&amp;focus=rustamid-imamate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
